{"type":"text","timestamp":1788198416509,"sessionID":"ses_fa71b57adffe2ekWe4aNvJvuc4","part":{"id":"prt_058ee66be001546DFulmaBUmD7","messageID":"msg_058edea940016HNssUeyty6MYC","sessionID":"ses_fa71b57adffe2ekWe4aNvJvuc4","type":"text","text":"The 413 path closes the connection before the client finishes sending the 1MB body, so urllib hits a broken pipe. I'll drain the oversized body (bounded) before responding so clients can read the 413.","time":{"start":1788198414014,"end":1788198416506}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788198416518,"sessionID":"ses_fa71b57adffe2ekWe4aNvJvuc4","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_0ab4454e6cf643a9a8f98365","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-d4c726zc/retort-013d5f93d56d/app.py","oldString":"        if length > self.max_body_bytes:\n            self.close_connection = True\n            raise ApiError(413, \"request body too large\")","newString":"        if length > self.max_body_bytes:\n            if not self._drain_body(length):\n                self.close_connection = True\n            raise ApiError(413, \"request body too large\")"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-d4c726zc/retort-013d5f93d56d/app.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-d4c726zc/retort-013d5f93d56d/app.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-d4c726zc/retort-013d5f93d56d/app.py\n@@ -276,9 +276,10 @@\n if length < 0:\n     self.close_connection = True\n     raise ApiError(400, \"invalid content-length header\")\n if length > self.max_body_bytes:\n-    self.close_connection = True\n+    if not self._drain_body(length):\n+        self.close_connection = True\n     raise ApiError(413, \"request body too large\")\n raw = self.rfile.read(length) if length else b\"\"\n if not raw:\n     return None\n","filediff":{"file":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-d4c726zc/retort-013d5f93d56d/app.py","patch":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-d4c726zc/retort-013d5f93d56d/app.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-d4c726zc/retort-013d5f93d56d/app.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-d4c726zc/retort-013d5f93d56d/app.py\n@@ -276,9 +276,10 @@\n if length < 0:\n     self.close_connection = True\n     raise ApiError(400, \"invalid content-length header\")\n if length > self.max_body_bytes:\n-    self.close_connection = True\n+    if not self._drain_body(length):\n+        self.close_connection = True\n     raise ApiError(413, \"request body too large\")\n raw = self.rfile.read(length) if length else b\"\"\n if not raw:\n     return None\n","additions":2,"deletions":1},"truncated":false},"title":"app.py","time":{"start":1788198416512,"end":1788198416515}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"43 passed, 1 failed: the 413 test. The server responds 413 and closes the connection before reading the full body → client gets broken pipe while still sending the 1MB body. The server correctly returned 413, but the client's write fails before it can read the response.\n\nThis is a classic early-response vs. large-body race. Options:\n\n1. In the 413 case, drain the body up to some larger bound before responding. But if body is huge, that's bad. Common servers just close the connection — and the client sees a connection error. curl handles this fine (it reads response after send fails sometimes); urllib raises URLError on broken pipe.\n\n2. Make the test tolerant: catch URLError/BrokenPipeError and accept that as 413-equivalent. But then the test wouldn't verify the status code.\n\n3. For the 413 path: set close_connection=True but ALSO drain the remaining body (read in chunks up to some drain limit, say 10MB) before responding. Then the client can finish sending and read the 413 response. Since max_body_bytes is 1MB, a malicious giant body would be drained up to drain limit... that reintroduces read-everything cost, bounded at, say, 16MB then hard close.\n\nActually a cleaner approach used by real servers: respond 413 and close. Clients that stream get an error — that's RFC-acceptable behavior. For the test, I could use a raw socket: send headers, read the 413 response, then close. But simpler: in the handler, after deciding 413, read and discard the body in chunks (bounded — cap total drain at, say, 64MB) so the client can complete its send and receive the response. Let me implement a drain:\n\n```python\nif length > self.max_body_bytes:\n    self._drain(length)  # bounded\n    self.close_connection = True\n    raise ApiError(413, ...)\n```\n\nHmm wait — if I drain the full body, do I even need close_connection? I could keep the connection alive after draining. But draining unbounded bodies is a DoS vector. Compromise: drain up to some limit (e.g., 64 MiB); beyond that, close mid-body (client gets broken pipe, which is fine for truly enormous bodies).\n\nActually — for this exercise, the simplest robust fix that keeps the test meaningful: drain the body (it's already bounded by a sane drain cap) and respond 413 with connection kept in sync. Let me set drain cap = 64 MiB. length can be up to... Content-Length could claim 10GB; drain reads until cap then closes.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\ndef _drain_body(self, remaining: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Read and discard up to `remaining` bytes; return False if the body\n    exceeded the drain cap (connection must be closed).\"\"\"\n    drain_cap = 64 * 1024 * 1024\n    to_read = min(remaining, drain_cap)\n    while to_read > 0:\n        chunk = self.rfile.read(min(65536, to_read))\n        if not chunk:\n            break\n        to_read -= len(chunk)\n    return remaining <= drain_cap\n```\n\nIn _read_body 413 branch:\n\n```python\nif length > self.max_body_bytes:\n    drained_ok = self._drain_body(length)\n    if not drained_ok:\n        self.close_connection = True\n    raise ApiError(413, \"request body too large\")\n```\n\nWith the 1MB+1 test body, drain succeeds, response sent, urllib reads 413. \n\nAlso same treatment for 411 (chunked) — we can't easily drain chunked; close. Fine.\n\nAnd \"invalid content-length\" — can't know body size; close (already do).\n\nLet me update app.py accordingly. Also keep `self.close_connection = True` when drain cap exceeded.\n\nEdge: drain when client uses HTTP/1.1 keep-alive and we respond 413 then close anyway? If drained fully, connection can stay open safely. I'll close only when not fully drained. With urllib (Connection: close by default), no issue either way.\n\nUpdate the test? No, test stays as is — it should now pass.\n\nLet me edit app.py.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_058ee7066001y8C3L7B4MO6bo8","sessionID":"ses_fa71b57adffe2ekWe4aNvJvuc4","messageID":"msg_058edea940016HNssUeyty6MYC"}}
